#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184926} :activity/id #uuid "e8299244-5795-54e0-9a37-3257c1c721cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048287 :topic/id #uuid "9128f088-7c43-5d28-a12d-6937728d8449" :topic/math-academy-id 3830} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185380 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function to be invertible on an interval, it must be one-to-one on the interval. So, to calculate the inverse of a quadratic function, we must first restrict its domain.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-2)^2-3\n$$\n\nSince the function is in vertex form, we can immediately see that the vertex of the corresponding parabola $y=f(x)$ is located at $(2,-3)$. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ will be one-to-one if we restrict its domain to either\n\n$$\nx\\leq 2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx\\geq 2\n$$\n\nLet's restrict the domain to\n\n$$\nx\\geq 2\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ for\n\n$$\nx\\geq 2\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/05/059fc0ffddc4b590de8597d3252a6901c7da16063e16b2b4a9a7b24383ce83f7.png)\n\nNow that $f(x)$ is one-to-one, we can calculate its inverse. We follow the four usual steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$.\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^2 - 3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$.\n\n$$\nx = (y-2)^2 - 3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (y - 2)^{2} - 3 \\\\\nx + 3 &= (y - 2)^{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{x + 3} &= y - 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{x + 3} + 2 &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The *domain* of the original function is the *range* of the inverse function. In our case, since the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$\n\nthe range of the inverse must be\n\n$$\nf^{-1} \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we select the positive square root in the third step above. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x + 3} +2 \\geq 2\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x + 3} +2\n$$\n\nWe're done!" :tutorial/id #uuid "783dd423-f37c-5794-a3be-b018f146724c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9258 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c88a3ac6-6ff0-5d00-811e-a83529593d3d" :step/math-academy-id 171319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183952 :question/id #uuid "af43cc58-820d-5f4c-9ab8-e3a8c05bcee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14873"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86cc32d1-5444-5deb-9168-ba67cd8d0e69" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050322}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184790}] :knowledge-point/title "Quadratic Functions Containing an Isolated Perfect Square"} :step/id #uuid "a55aa1a1-9ecd-55c7-8081-bd95dc5783c8" :step/math-academy-id 171320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183953 :question/id #uuid "28859a1c-7f0d-58f2-8c77-ff4e27cf610d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14877"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50718b79-529c-5515-bfa2-dbb8b97f4a4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184791}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse by Completing the Square"} :step/id #uuid "a8bc7ddf-b8ce-5078-b12f-c1d1a86cd036" :step/math-academy-id 171323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184166 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183954 :question/id #uuid "836ac184-e55d-5c32-a4af-8a0da847a423" :question/math-academy-id "e-14879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02655639-b390-510d-9eda-3221375e7e5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592